llama.cpp (as fetched by Ollama v0.35.1, llama.cpp b11232, applied after
117): MoE expert matmuls of prompt batches with Q4_K and Q6_K weights run
as a grouped fp32 GEMM that dequantizes the weights on their way into
shared memory, instead of mmq.
gfx803-specific: engages only on GCN4 (cc == GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4).

WHY
---
In a 2048-token prompt batch of Qwen3.6 35B-A3B (Q4_K_M; 256 experts, 8 per
token, expert ffn 512, hidden 2048) on an RX 590, the 120 MUL_MAT_ID calls
(gate, up and down in 40 layers) take about 2.03 s of the batch's 3.26 s of
GPU kernel time, 62%. Each call is 34.4 GFLOP, and mul_mat_q runs a Q4_K
call in 16.3 ms (2.1 TFLOPS) and a Q6_K call in 21.7 ms (1.6 TFLOPS).
gfx803 has no integer dot instruction: mmq builds each int8 dot product
from byte extracts and 24-bit multiply-adds, about three instructions per
multiply-add, after quantizing the activations to 8 bits. The card's fp32
multiply-add (v_mac_f32) runs at one per lane per clock, and the base
image's gfx803 SGEMM reaches 3.8-4.8 TFLOPS with it; patch 106 already
sends dense prompt matmuls there. MUL_MAT_ID cannot take that route: every
expert gets a different, data-dependent set of tokens (64 on average at
2048 tokens) whose counts exist only on the device, so one GEMM per expert
would need a copy to the host and a stream synchronization per call.

GPUs with a native int8 dot instruction (dp4a, v_dot4, MMA) run mmq's
products at full rate and keep it; the kernel is only engaged on GCN4,
the same host test patch 100 uses for its gfx803 tile table.

WHAT
----
New mmid-f32.cuh; ggml_cuda_mul_mat_q tries ggml_cuda_mmid_f32 first for
calls with expert ids.
1. mm_ids_helper sorts the token slots by expert as before (forward map).
   A one-block kernel turns its expert bounds into a tile list on the
   device: one entry per expert and run of up to 64 tokens. The GEMM is
   launched for the largest tile count the call can have, (slots +
   experts*63)/64 times ne01/128 blocks, and blocks past the real count
   exit at once. No host synchronization, so HIP graphs still capture the
   call. Blocks go out by expert, then token tile, then row tile, so
   concurrent blocks share an expert's weights and a token tile's
   activations in L2.
2. mmid_f32_gemm: a block of 4 waves computes 128 weight rows x 64 tokens
   of one expert, 32 K values per stage (one Q4_K sub-block, one Q6_K pair
   of 16-value scales). Two threads per row dequantize its 32 values
   straight from the quantized block into fp32 shared memory (Q4_K: the
   16-byte header and 16 quant bytes; Q6_K: 16 low-bit bytes, 16 high-bit
   bytes, the int8 scale and d), next to the fp32 activation rows gathered
   through ids_src1. The global loads for the next stage are issued before
   the current stage's multiply-adds and dequantized after them. A wave
   owns 16 tokens and each lane an 8x4 block of outputs, so a K step is
   three 16-byte LDS reads and 32 v_mac_f32. Results go to dst through
   ids_dst.
3. Most experts' last tile holds fewer than 64 tokens. With 1-16 tokens
   the four waves split each stage's 32 K values four ways, with 17-32 two
   ways, and add their partial sums through shared memory at the end, so
   every SIMD keeps doing useful multiply-adds.
4. The kernel takes a call when the batch averages at least 16 tokens per
   expert (512 tokens for this model; below that most tiles hold one
   partly filled group of 16 and the per-tile dequantization outweighs
   the gain), the weights are Q4_K or Q6_K with ne00 a multiple of 256 and
   ne01 a multiple of 128, and src1/dst are f32 with 16-byte aligned rows.
   Everything else, decode included, runs as before.

gfx803 details. The nibble masks pass through an empty asm statement: with
the constants visible LLVM emits v_bfe_u32 + v_cvt_f32_ubyte0 per value,
without them one v_cvt_f32_ubyteN per value. Dequantization is one
v_mad_f32 per value and the multiply-adds are v_mac_f32 only; there is no
v_fma_f32 (a quarter-rate instruction on gfx803) in the kernels.
block_q6_K is 210 bytes, so half of its blocks start 2 bytes off a 4-byte
boundary; their 16-byte loads stay single loads because gfx8 runs in
unaligned mode under amdkfd, as the base image's SGEMM also relies on.
Both kernels use 112-115 VGPRs, no scratch and 24 KB of shared memory: two
blocks, two waves per SIMD, per CU. In a full tile 93% (Q4_K) and 91%
(Q6_K) of the VALU instructions are the multiply-adds.

Measured on an RX 590. test-backend-ops MUL_MAT_ID passes 953 of 953
cases against the CPU backend, including cases added for the check at
Qwen3.6 35B-A3B's expert shapes (256 experts, 8 used, 512 and 2048
tokens) with uniform and skewed routing (a few experts get most tokens,
many get none or one), and small and strided-expert shapes. Op time
against mmq at those shapes: gate/up Q4_K (512 x 2048) 1.56-1.70x faster,
down Q4_K (2048 x 512) 2.45-2.65x, down Q6_K 1.87x at 2048 tokens and
1.31-1.62x at 512.

llama-bench, Qwen3.6 35B-A3B Q4_K_M, all experts in system RAM with
-ncmoe 99, page cache dropped before each run, HSA_ENABLE_SDMA=1
ROC_CPU_WAIT_FOR_SIGNAL=1, runs alternating with and without the patch:
pp2048 (2048-token batches) 447-451 -> 546-548 tok/s (+21%), pp512
233-235 -> 274-275 tok/s (+17%), tg128 unchanged.

Numerics: results are not bit-identical to mmq. mmq quantizes the
activations to q8_1 before its integer products; this kernel keeps them in
fp32 and dequantizes the weights as ggml's CPU code does (each value one
non-fused multiply-add), so it lands closer to the CPU reference. Against
CPU-computed logits (llama-perplexity, 8 chunks of 512 tokens in
512-token batches, q8_0 KV cache): mean KLD 0.00965 +- 0.00042 with this
patch and 0.01070 +- 0.00051 with mmq, PPL 13.350 and 13.384 (CPU
13.355), same top token 95.64% and 95.29%.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmid-f32.cuh b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmid-f32.cuh
new file mode 100644
--- /dev/null
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-cuda/mmid-f32.cuh
@@ -0,0 +1,419 @@
+#pragma once
+
+// Grouped fp32 GEMM for MUL_MAT_ID prompt batches with Q4_K or Q6_K experts on
+// GCN4 (gfx803 class), which has no int8 dot instruction for mmq.
+
+#include "common.cuh"
+#include "mmid.cuh"
+
+#define MMID_F32_BM 128 // weight rows (dst columns) per tile
+#define MMID_F32_NW 4   // waves per block, 16 tokens each
+#define MMID_F32_KT 32  // K values per LDS stage: one Q4_K sub-block, one Q6_K scale pair
+
+// Below this many tokens per expert on average most tiles hold one partly
+// filled wave of 16 tokens, so the dequantization, done once per tile, is
+// spread over too few multiply-adds to beat mmq.
+#define MMID_F32_MIN_AVG_TOKENS 16
+
+// One entry per (expert, run of up to bn tokens): x = expert, y = first row of
+// the expert's slice in ids_src1/ids_dst, z = token count.
+static __global__ void mmid_f32_tile_list(const int32_t * __restrict__ expert_bounds, const int n_experts, const int bn,
+                                          int4 * __restrict__ tiles, int * __restrict__ n_tiles) {
+    __shared__ int scan[256];
+    int base = 0;
+    for (int e0 = 0; e0 < n_experts; e0 += 256) {
+        const int e = e0 + threadIdx.x;
+        int b0 = 0;
+        int n  = 0;
+        if (e < n_experts) {
+            b0 = expert_bounds[e];
+            n  = expert_bounds[e + 1] - b0;
+        }
+        const int cnt = (n + bn - 1) / bn;
+        scan[threadIdx.x] = cnt;
+        __syncthreads();
+        for (int off = 1; off < 256; off <<= 1) {
+            const int v = threadIdx.x >= off ? scan[threadIdx.x - off] : 0;
+            __syncthreads();
+            scan[threadIdx.x] += v;
+            __syncthreads();
+        }
+        const int start = base + scan[threadIdx.x] - cnt;
+        for (int t = 0; t < cnt; ++t) {
+            tiles[start + t] = make_int4(e, b0 + t*bn, min(bn, n - t*bn), 0);
+        }
+        base += scan[255];
+        __syncthreads();
+    }
+    if (threadIdx.x == 0) {
+        *n_tiles = base;
+    }
+}
+
+static __device__ __forceinline__ float mmid_f32_half(const uint32_t bits) {
+    return (float) __builtin_bit_cast(_Float16, (uint16_t) bits);
+}
+
+static __device__ __forceinline__ uint32_t mmid_f32_u8(const uint32_t w, const int i) {
+    return (w >> (8*i)) & 0xFF;
+}
+
+// With the 0x0F0F0F0F nibble masks visible, LLVM turns every weight value into
+// v_bfe_u32 + v_cvt_f32_ubyte0; with the masks opaque one v_cvt_f32_ubyte<e>
+// per value remains, a third fewer instructions in the dequantization.
+static __device__ __forceinline__ uint32_t mmid_f32_opaque(uint32_t v) {
+#if defined(__HIP_DEVICE_COMPILE__)
+    asm volatile("" : "+v"(v));
+#endif // defined(__HIP_DEVICE_COMPILE__)
+    return v;
+}
+
+// Weight values of q (its four bytes) for K positions 4*i..4*i+3 of the thread.
+static __device__ __forceinline__ void mmid_f32_put4(float * v, const int i, const uint32_t q, const float s, const float c) {
+    v[4*i + 0] = (float) ((q >>  0) & 0xFF)*s + c;
+    v[4*i + 1] = (float) ((q >>  8) & 0xFF)*s + c;
+    v[4*i + 2] = (float) ((q >> 16) & 0xFF)*s + c;
+    v[4*i + 3] = (float) ((q >> 24) & 0xFF)*s + c;
+}
+
+// The weight operand of one stage for one thread: VPT consecutive K values of
+// one row, starting at value VPT*part of the 32-value stage. load() issues the
+// global loads; dequant() runs after the stage's multiply-adds, when they have
+// arrived. A stage is one Q4_K sub-block, or one Q6_K group with two 16-value
+// scales, so the scale selection is uniform across the block.
+template <ggml_type type, int VPT> struct mmid_f32_wq;
+
+template <int VPT> struct mmid_f32_wq<GGML_TYPE_Q4_K, VPT> {
+    static constexpr int NQ = VPT/16;
+    uint4 hdr; // d, dmin, scales[12]
+    uint4 qs[NQ];
+
+    __device__ __forceinline__ void load(const char * xr, const int k0, const int part) {
+        const char * b = xr + (k0/QK_K)*sizeof(block_q4_K);
+        const int    j = (k0 % QK_K)/64;
+        hdr = *(const uint4 *) b;
+#pragma unroll
+        for (int i = 0; i < NQ; ++i) {
+            qs[i] = *(const uint4 *) (b + 16 + 32*j + VPT*part + 16*i);
+        }
+    }
+
+    __device__ __forceinline__ void dequant(const int k0, const int part, const uint32_t m4, const uint32_t m2, float v[VPT]) const {
+        GGML_UNUSED(part);
+        GGML_UNUSED(m2);
+        const int sb = (k0 % QK_K)/32;
+        const int sh = 4*(sb % 2);
+        const float d    = mmid_f32_half(hdr.x);
+        const float dmin = mmid_f32_half(hdr.x >> 16);
+        uint32_t sc;
+        uint32_t m;
+        if (sb < 4) {
+            sc = mmid_f32_u8(hdr.y, sb) & 63;
+            m  = mmid_f32_u8(hdr.z, sb) & 63;
+        } else {
+            sc = (mmid_f32_u8(hdr.w, sb - 4) & 0xF) | ((mmid_f32_u8(hdr.y, sb - 4) >> 6) << 4);
+            m  = (mmid_f32_u8(hdr.w, sb - 4) >>  4) | ((mmid_f32_u8(hdr.z, sb - 4) >> 6) << 4);
+        }
+        const float s  = d*(float) sc;
+        const float mn = -(dmin*(float) m);
+#pragma unroll
+        for (int i = 0; i < NQ; ++i) {
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 0, (qs[i].x >> sh) & m4, s, mn);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 1, (qs[i].y >> sh) & m4, s, mn);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 2, (qs[i].z >> sh) & m4, s, mn);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 3, (qs[i].w >> sh) & m4, s, mn);
+        }
+    }
+};
+
+// block_q6_K is 210 bytes, so every other block starts 2 bytes past a 4-byte
+// boundary. gfx8 runs with SH_MEM_CONFIG in unaligned mode under amdkfd, so
+// these stay single wide loads (the gfx803 SGEMM in the base image relies on
+// the same).
+template <int VPT> struct mmid_f32_wq<GGML_TYPE_Q6_K, VPT> {
+    static constexpr int NQ = VPT/16;
+    uint4    ql[NQ];
+    uint4    qh[NQ];
+    uint32_t sc; // the int8 scales of the thread's values: one per 16
+    uint32_t d;
+
+    __device__ __forceinline__ void load(const char * xr, const int k0, const int part) {
+        const char * b = xr + (k0/QK_K)*sizeof(block_q6_K);
+        const int    w = k0 % QK_K;
+        const int    n = w/128;
+        const int    g = (w % 128)/32;
+#pragma unroll
+        for (int i = 0; i < NQ; ++i) {
+            ql[i] = *(const uint4 *) (b + 64*n + 32*(g % 2) + VPT*part + 16*i);
+            qh[i] = *(const uint4 *) (b + QK_K/2 + 32*n + VPT*part + 16*i);
+        }
+        if constexpr (NQ == 2) {
+            sc = *(const uint16_t *) (b + QK_K/2 + QK_K/4 + 8*n + 2*g);
+        } else {
+            sc = *(const uint8_t *) (b + QK_K/2 + QK_K/4 + 8*n + 2*g + part);
+        }
+        d = *(const uint16_t *) (b + QK_K/2 + QK_K/4 + QK_K/16);
+    }
+
+    __device__ __forceinline__ void dequant(const int k0, const int part, const uint32_t m4, const uint32_t m2, float v[VPT]) const {
+        GGML_UNUSED(part);
+        const int g   = (k0 % 128)/32;
+        const int shl = 4*(g/2);
+        const int shh = 2*g;
+        const float dd = mmid_f32_half(d);
+#pragma unroll
+        for (int i = 0; i < NQ; ++i) {
+            const float s = dd*(float) (int8_t) mmid_f32_u8(sc, i);
+            const float c = -32.0f*s;
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 0, ((ql[i].x >> shl) & m4) | (((qh[i].x >> shh) & m2) << 4), s, c);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 1, ((ql[i].y >> shl) & m4) | (((qh[i].y >> shh) & m2) << 4), s, c);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 2, ((ql[i].z >> shl) & m4) | (((qh[i].z >> shh) & m2) << 4), s, c);
+            mmid_f32_put4(v, 4*i + 3, ((ql[i].w >> shl) & m4) | (((qh[i].w >> shh) & m2) << 4), s, c);
+        }
+    }
+};
+
+// dst[ids_dst[j], mt*BM + r] = sum_k W_e[r, k] * src1[ids_src1[j], k] for the
+// tokens j of one tile entry. A block of NW waves computes 128 weight rows x
+// 16*NW tokens. A wave takes one group of 16 tokens for all 128 rows, each
+// lane an 8x4 block (rows 4*tr..+3 and 64+4*tr..+3, tokens 4*tc..+3), so per
+// K step a lane reads three float4 from LDS for 32 multiply-adds. Most experts
+// get fewer tokens than a tile holds; then the waves of a token group split
+// each stage's K range (ns slices) and add their sums through LDS at the end,
+// so that every SIMD keeps working instead of one wave per tile. Each row is
+// dequantized by NW/2 threads, VPT values each.
+template <ggml_type type>
+static __global__ void __launch_bounds__(64*MMID_F32_NW, 2) mmid_f32_gemm(
+        const char * __restrict__ x, const int64_t nb01, const int64_t nb02,
+        const char * __restrict__ y, const int64_t nb11,
+        const int32_t * __restrict__ ids_src1, const int32_t * __restrict__ ids_dst,
+        const int4 * __restrict__ tiles, const int * __restrict__ n_tiles,
+        float * __restrict__ dst, const int64_t s1, const int K, const int m_tiles) {
+    constexpr int NW  = MMID_F32_NW;
+    constexpr int BM  = MMID_F32_BM;
+    constexpr int BN  = 16*NW;
+    constexpr int KT  = MMID_F32_KT;
+    constexpr int VPT = 2*KT/NW;
+    __shared__ float smem[KT*BM + KT*BN];
+    static_assert((NW - 1)*32*64 <= KT*BM + KT*BN, "partial sums do not fit");
+    float (*As)[BM] = (float (*)[BM]) smem;
+    float (*Bs)[BN] = (float (*)[BN]) (smem + KT*BM);
+
+    const int nt = blockIdx.x / m_tiles;
+    const int mt = blockIdx.x - nt*m_tiles;
+    if (nt >= *n_tiles) {
+        return;
+    }
+    const int4 tile = tiles[nt];
+    const int  nj   = tile.z;
+
+    const int tid  = threadIdx.x;
+    const int row  = tid % BM;
+    const int part = tid / BM;
+    const int tok  = tid % BN;
+    const int kg   = tid / BN;
+    const char * xr = x + tile.x*nb02 + (int64_t) (mt*BM + row)*nb01;
+    // Columns past nj read a valid row; their outputs are never stored.
+    const float * yr = (const float *) (y + (int64_t) ids_src1[tile.y + min(tok, nj - 1)]*nb11) + 8*kg;
+
+    const int ngroups = nj <= 16 ? 1 : nj <= 32 ? 2 : 4;
+    const int ns      = NW/ngroups;
+    const int lane    = tid % 64;
+    const int wave    = __builtin_amdgcn_readfirstlane(tid / 64);
+    const int tg      = wave / ns;
+    const int ks      = wave % ns;
+    const int tr      = lane % 16;
+    const int tc      = lane / 16;
+    const bool active = 16*tg < nj;
+
+    mmid_f32_wq<type, VPT> wq;
+    float4 ya;
+    float4 yb;
+    auto fetch = [&](const int k0) {
+        wq.load(xr, k0, part);
+        ya = *(const float4 *) (yr + k0);
+        yb = *(const float4 *) (yr + k0 + 4);
+    };
+
+    float acc[8][4];
+#pragma unroll
+    for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
+#pragma unroll
+        for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+            acc[i][j] = 0.0f;
+        }
+    }
+
+    const uint32_t m4 = mmid_f32_opaque(0x0F0F0F0F);
+    const uint32_t m2 = mmid_f32_opaque(0x03030303);
+
+    fetch(0);
+    for (int k0 = 0; k0 < K; k0 += KT) {
+        {
+            float v[VPT];
+            wq.dequant(k0, part, m4, m2, v);
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int k = 0; k < VPT; ++k) {
+                As[VPT*part + k][row] = v[k];
+            }
+            Bs[8*kg + 0][tok] = ya.x;
+            Bs[8*kg + 1][tok] = ya.y;
+            Bs[8*kg + 2][tok] = ya.z;
+            Bs[8*kg + 3][tok] = ya.w;
+            Bs[8*kg + 4][tok] = yb.x;
+            Bs[8*kg + 5][tok] = yb.y;
+            Bs[8*kg + 6][tok] = yb.z;
+            Bs[8*kg + 7][tok] = yb.w;
+        }
+        __syncthreads();
+        if (k0 + KT < K) {
+            fetch(k0 + KT);
+        }
+        auto step = [&](const int k) {
+            const float4 a0 = *(const float4 *) &As[k][4*tr];
+            const float4 a1 = *(const float4 *) &As[k][64 + 4*tr];
+            const float4 b  = *(const float4 *) &Bs[k][16*tg + 4*tc];
+            const float av[8] = {a0.x, a0.y, a0.z, a0.w, a1.x, a1.y, a1.z, a1.w};
+            const float bv[4] = {b.x, b.y, b.z, b.w};
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
+#pragma unroll
+                for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+                    acc[i][j] += av[i]*bv[j];
+                }
+            }
+        };
+        // Full tiles take the straight 32-step sequence, so the LDS reads of
+        // each step are issued during the previous step's multiply-adds.
+        if (ns == 1) {
+            if (active) {
+#pragma unroll
+                for (int k = 0; k < KT; ++k) {
+                    step(k);
+                }
+            }
+        } else {
+            for (int kc = ks*(KT/ns); kc < (ks + 1)*(KT/ns); kc += 8) {
+#pragma unroll
+                for (int kk = 0; kk < 8; ++kk) {
+                    step(kc + kk);
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        __syncthreads();
+    }
+
+    if (ns > 1) {
+        // Lane-contiguous, so each of the 32 stores and loads per lane covers
+        // 64 consecutive words: no bank conflicts.
+        float * red = smem + (tg*(ns - 1) + ks - 1)*(32*64) + lane;
+        if (ks > 0 && active) {
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
+#pragma unroll
+                for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+                    red[(4*i + j)*64] = acc[i][j];
+                }
+            }
+        }
+        __syncthreads();
+        if (ks > 0) {
+            return;
+        }
+        for (int p = 1; p < ns; ++p) {
+            const float * part_sum = smem + (tg*(ns - 1) + p - 1)*(32*64) + lane;
+#pragma unroll
+            for (int i = 0; i < 8; ++i) {
+#pragma unroll
+                for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+                    acc[i][j] += part_sum[(4*i + j)*64];
+                }
+            }
+        }
+    }
+
+    if (!active) {
+        return;
+    }
+#pragma unroll
+    for (int j = 0; j < 4; ++j) {
+        const int t = 16*tg + 4*tc + j;
+        if (t < nj) {
+            float * d = dst + (int64_t) ids_dst[tile.y + t]*s1 + mt*BM;
+            *(float4 *) (d +      4*tr) = make_float4(acc[0][j], acc[1][j], acc[2][j], acc[3][j]);
+            *(float4 *) (d + 64 + 4*tr) = make_float4(acc[4][j], acc[5][j], acc[6][j], acc[7][j]);
+        }
+    }
+}
+
+// Runs MUL_MAT_ID as the grouped fp32 GEMM and returns true, or returns false
+// and leaves the call to mmq.
+static bool ggml_cuda_mmid_f32(ggml_backend_cuda_context & ctx, const ggml_tensor * src0, const ggml_tensor * src1,
+                               const ggml_tensor * ids, ggml_tensor * dst) {
+    // With a native int8 dot instruction mmq's quantized products run at full
+    // rate and stay ahead of fp32 tiles; GCN4 emulates them at ~3 VALU each.
+    const int cc = ggml_cuda_info().devices[ggml_cuda_get_device()].cc;
+    if (cc != GGML_CUDA_CC_GCN4) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    GGML_TENSOR_BINARY_OP_LOCALS;
+
+    const int64_t n_expert_used = ids->ne[0];
+    const int64_t ne_get_rows   = ne12*n_expert_used;
+    if (ne_get_rows < MMID_F32_MIN_AVG_TOKENS*ne02) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    const bool supported = (src0->type == GGML_TYPE_Q4_K || src0->type == GGML_TYPE_Q6_K) &&
+        nb00 == ggml_type_size(src0->type) && ne00 % QK_K == 0 && ne01 % MMID_F32_BM == 0 &&
+        ne03 == 1 && ne13 == 1 && ne1 == n_expert_used && ids->nb[0] == sizeof(int32_t) &&
+        src1->type == GGML_TYPE_F32 && nb10 == sizeof(float) && nb11 % 16 == 0 && nb12 % nb11 == 0 &&
+        (uintptr_t) src1->data % 16 == 0 && dst->type == GGML_TYPE_F32 && ggml_is_contiguous(dst) &&
+        (uintptr_t) dst->data % 16 == 0 && ne_get_rows < (1 << 22);
+    if (!supported) {
+        return false;
+    }
+
+    cudaStream_t stream = ctx.stream();
+
+    ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<int32_t> ids_src1(ctx.pool(), ne_get_rows);
+    ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<int32_t> ids_dst(ctx.pool(), ne_get_rows);
+    ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<int32_t> expert_bounds(ctx.pool(), ne02 + 1);
+
+    const int     bn        = 16*MMID_F32_NW;
+    const int64_t max_tiles = (ne_get_rows + ne02*(bn - 1))/bn;
+    ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<int4> tiles(ctx.pool(), max_tiles);
+    ggml_cuda_pool_alloc<int>  n_tiles(ctx.pool(), 1);
+
+    {
+        const int si1  = ids->nb[1] / ggml_element_size(ids);
+        const int sis1 = nb12 / nb11;
+        ggml_cuda_launch_mm_ids_helper((const int32_t *) ids->data, ids_src1.get(), ids_dst.get(), expert_bounds.get(),
+            ne02, ne12, n_expert_used, ne11, si1, sis1, /*write_inverse =*/ false, stream);
+        CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
+    }
+
+    mmid_f32_tile_list<<<1, 256, 0, stream>>>(expert_bounds.get(), ne02, bn, tiles.get(), n_tiles.get());
+    CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
+
+    // The tile count exists only on the device; launching for the largest
+    // possible count, with surplus blocks exiting at once, avoids a host sync.
+    const int     m_tiles  = ne01 / MMID_F32_BM;
+    const int64_t n_blocks = max_tiles*m_tiles;
+    GGML_ASSERT(n_blocks < INT_MAX);
+
+    const char * x = (const char *) src0->data;
+    const char * y = (const char *) src1->data;
+    float      * d = (float *) dst->data;
+    const int64_t s1 = nb1 / sizeof(float);
+    if (src0->type == GGML_TYPE_Q4_K) {
+        mmid_f32_gemm<GGML_TYPE_Q4_K><<<n_blocks, 64*MMID_F32_NW, 0, stream>>>(x, nb01, nb02, y, nb11,
+            ids_src1.get(), ids_dst.get(), tiles.get(), n_tiles.get(), d, s1, ne00, m_tiles);
+    } else {
+        mmid_f32_gemm<GGML_TYPE_Q6_K><<<n_blocks, 64*MMID_F32_NW, 0, stream>>>(x, nb01, nb02, y, nb11,
+            ids_src1.get(), ids_dst.get(), tiles.get(), n_tiles.get(), d, s1, ne00, m_tiles);
+    }
+    CUDA_CHECK(cudaGetLastError());
+    return true;
+}
